Relevant columns and how they might be used  

• WORLD_PORT_INDEX  
  – "region_number": filter to the requested region (= '6585').  
  – "port_latitude", "port_longitude": create a point geometry for spatial joins.  
  – "port_geom": WKB geometry, can be cast to GEOGRAPHY.  
  – "port_name": output.  
  – "country": keep only rows = 'US'.  

• STATES  
  – "state_name", "state": supply the full state name / code.  
  – "state_geom": polygon geometry that defines the state boundary.  

• HURRICANES  
  – "basin": restrict to 'NA' (North Atlantic).  
  – "usa_wind": filter ≥ 35 kt.  
  – "usa_sshs": ≥ 0 (minimal tropical‑storm strength).  
  – "name": only named storms (`"name" NOT ILIKE 'NOT%'`).  
  – "season": year of the storm.  
  – "latitude", "longitude": centre position of each 6‑hour fix; can be converted to a point geometry for distance checks.  
  – "sid": unique storm id (identifies one named storm).  
  – "usa_sshs", "usa_wind": later averaged for the report.  

Typical conditions that will be needed  

1. Ports in requested region: "region_number" = '6585'.  
2. Ports inside a U.S. state: ST_WITHIN(TO_GEOGRAPHY("port_geom"), TO_GEOGRAPHY("state_geom")).  
3. North‑Atlantic named storms of at least TS strength:  
   "basin" = 'NA' AND "usa_wind" >= 35 AND "usa_sshs" >= 0 AND "name" NOT ILIKE 'NOT%'.  
4. Storm “affects” port: distance between port point and storm fix point ≤ e.g. 100 km  
   (example: ST_DISTANCE("port_geog", "storm_geog") <= 100000).  

Below are ten exploratory Snowflake SQL queries (all limited to 20 rows, increasing in complexity) that lead up to the final answer.  
Every identifier is double‑quoted; each query is preceded by an explanation.

```sql
-- 1) Description: Quick look at ports that belong to region 6585
SELECT DISTINCT
       "index_number",
       "port_name",
       "country",
       "region_number"
FROM   NOAA_PORTS.GEO_INTERNATIONAL_PORTS.WORLD_PORT_INDEX
WHERE  "region_number" = '6585'
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 2) Description: Inspect which of those ports are flagged as U.S. (“country” = 'US')
SELECT DISTINCT
       "index_number",
       "port_name",
       "country"
FROM   NOAA_PORTS.GEO_INTERNATIONAL_PORTS.WORLD_PORT_INDEX
WHERE  "region_number" = '6585'
  AND  "country" = 'US'
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 3) Description: List all U.S. state names and their two–letter codes
SELECT DISTINCT
       "state_fips_code",
       "state",
       "state_name"
FROM   NOAA_PORTS.GEO_US_BOUNDARIES.STATES
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 4) Description: Sample spatial join – first 20 ports from region 6585 and the state they fall in
SELECT p."port_name",
       s."state_name"
FROM   NOAA_PORTS.GEO_INTERNATIONAL_PORTS.WORLD_PORT_INDEX p
JOIN   NOAA_PORTS.GEO_US_BOUNDARIES.STATES s
       ON ST_WITHIN(TO_GEOGRAPHY(p."port_geom"), TO_GEOGRAPHY(s."state_geom"))
WHERE  p."region_number" = '6585'
  AND  p."country" = 'US'
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 5) Description: Preview North‑Atlantic named storms (≥35 kt, ≥TS) with basic fields
SELECT DISTINCT
       "sid",
       "name",
       "season",
       "usa_wind",
       "usa_sshs"
FROM   NOAA_PORTS.NOAA_HURRICANES.HURRICANES
WHERE  "basin" = 'NA'
  AND  "usa_wind" >= 35
  AND  "usa_sshs" >= 0
  AND  "name" NOT ILIKE 'NOT%'
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 6) Description: Turn each storm fix into a GEOGRAPHY point (first 20)
SELECT
       "sid",
       "name",
       "season",
       ST_POINT("longitude", "latitude")                  AS "storm_geog",
       "usa_wind",
       "usa_sshs"
FROM   NOAA_PORTS.NOAA_HURRICANES.HURRICANES
WHERE  "basin" = 'NA'
  AND  "usa_wind" >= 35
  AND  "usa_sshs" >= 0
  AND  "name" NOT ILIKE 'NOT%'
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 7) Description: Distance check – show a few matches where a storm fix lies within 100 km of a port
WITH ports AS (
  SELECT "index_number",
         "port_name",
         ST_POINT("port_longitude", "port_latitude") AS "port_geog"
  FROM   NOAA_PORTS.GEO_INTERNATIONAL_PORTS.WORLD_PORT_INDEX
  WHERE  "region_number" = '6585'
    AND  "country" = 'US'
)
SELECT p."port_name",
       h."name"          AS "storm_name",
       h."season",
       ST_DISTANCE(p."port_geog", ST_POINT(h."longitude", h."latitude")) AS "meters_to_fix"
FROM   ports p
JOIN   NOAA_PORTS.NOAA_HURRICANES.HURRICANES h
       ON h."basin"      = 'NA'
      AND h."usa_wind"   >= 35
      AND h."usa_sshs"   >= 0
      AND h."name"      NOT ILIKE 'NOT%'
      AND ST_DISTANCE(p."port_geog", ST_POINT(h."longitude", h."latitude")) <= 100000
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 8) Description: Attach state information to the port–storm pairs (still limited sample)
WITH ports AS (
  SELECT p."index_number",
         p."port_name",
         ST_POINT(p."port_longitude", p."port_latitude") AS "port_geog",
         s."state_name"
  FROM   NOAA_PORTS.GEO_INTERNATIONAL_PORTS.WORLD_PORT_INDEX p
  JOIN   NOAA_PORTS.GEO_US_BOUNDARIES.STATES s
         ON ST_WITHIN(TO_GEOGRAPHY(p."port_geom"), TO_GEOGRAPHY(s."state_geom"))
  WHERE  p."region_number" = '6585'
    AND  p."country" = 'US'
)
SELECT p."port_name",
       p."state_name",
       h."season",
       h."name"           AS "storm_name",
       h."usa_wind",
       h."usa_sshs"
FROM   ports p
JOIN   NOAA_PORTS.NOAA_HURRICANES.HURRICANES h
       ON h."basin"    = 'NA'
      AND h."usa_wind" >= 35
      AND h."usa_sshs" >= 0
      AND h."name"    NOT ILIKE 'NOT%'
      AND ST_DISTANCE(p."port_geog", ST_POINT(h."longitude", h."latitude")) <= 100000
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 9) Description: Aggregate – per port & storm count of distinct storms that met the criteria
WITH port_state AS (
  SELECT p."index_number",
         p."port_name",
         ST_POINT(p."port_longitude", p."port_latitude") AS "port_geog",
         s."state_name"
  FROM   NOAA_PORTS.GEO_INTERNATIONAL_PORTS.WORLD_PORT_INDEX p
  JOIN   NOAA_PORTS.GEO_US_BOUNDARIES.STATES s
         ON ST_WITHIN(TO_GEOGRAPHY(p."port_geom"), TO_GEOGRAPHY(s."state_geom"))
  WHERE  p."region_number" = '6585'
    AND  p."country" = 'US'
),
hits AS (
  SELECT DISTINCT
         ps."index_number",
         ps."port_name",
         ps."state_name",
         h."sid",
         h."name"     AS "storm_name",
         h."season",
         h."usa_wind",
         h."usa_sshs"
  FROM   port_state ps
  JOIN   NOAA_PORTS.NOAA_HURRICANES.HURRICANES h
         ON h."basin"    = 'NA'
        AND h."usa_wind" >= 35
        AND h."usa_sshs" >= 0
        AND h."name"    NOT ILIKE 'NOT%'
        AND ST_DISTANCE(ps."port_geog", ST_POINT(h."longitude", h."latitude")) <= 100000
)
SELECT
       "port_name",
       "state_name",
       COUNT(DISTINCT "sid") AS "distinct_storms"
FROM   hits
GROUP  BY 1,2
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 10) Description: Final all‑in‑one query (still limited to 20) – 
-- summarises years, storm list, avg category & wind, and returns geometries
WITH port_state AS (
  SELECT p."index_number",
         p."port_name",
         TO_GEOGRAPHY(p."port_geom")                               AS "port_geog",
         ST_POINT(p."port_longitude", p."port_latitude")           AS "port_centroid",
         s."state_name"
  FROM   NOAA_PORTS.GEO_INTERNATIONAL_PORTS.WORLD_PORT_INDEX p
  JOIN   NOAA_PORTS.GEO_US_BOUNDARIES.STATES s
         ON ST_WITHIN(TO_GEOGRAPHY(p."port_geom"), TO_GEOGRAPHY(s."state_geom"))
  WHERE  p."region_number" = '6585'
    AND  p."country" = 'US'
),
hits AS (
  SELECT DISTINCT
         ps."index_number",
         ps."port_name",
         ps."state_name",
         ps."port_geog",
         h."sid",
         h."name"           AS "storm_name",
         h."season",
         h."usa_wind",
         h."usa_sshs",
         ST_POINT(h."longitude", h."latitude")         AS "storm_geog"
  FROM   port_state ps
  JOIN   NOAA_PORTS.NOAA_HURRICANES.HURRICANES h
         ON h."basin"    = 'NA'
        AND h."usa_wind" >= 35
        AND h."usa_sshs" >= 0
        AND h."name"    NOT ILIKE 'NOT%'
        AND ST_DISTANCE(ps."port_centroid", ST_POINT(h."longitude", h."latitude")) <= 100000
)
SELECT
       "port_name",
       "state_name",
       ARRAY_AGG(DISTINCT "season")                      AS "storm_years",
       COUNT(DISTINCT "sid")                             AS "total_distinct_storms",
       ARRAY_AGG(DISTINCT "storm_name")                  AS "storm_names",
       AVG("usa_sshs")                                   AS "avg_sshs_category",
       AVG("usa_wind")                                   AS "avg_wind_knots",
       "port_geog",
       ARRAY_AGG(DISTINCT "storm_geog")                  AS "storm_points"
FROM   hits
GROUP  BY "port_name", "state_name", "port_geog"
LIMIT  20;
```

The sequence moves from basic inspection to the fully articulated solution, demonstrating how each table and column is progressively employed.
